  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining CSS Rules**\n\nI\'m currently focused on customizing the CSS, specifically within `src/index.css`. I\'ve noted the existing Tailwind import. Now, I\'m working on defining explicit CSS rules for `.card`, `.card::after`, and `.card::before`. My goal is to also ensure a pure white background (`#ffffff`) for `html` and `body`.\n\n\n**Refining CSS Parameters**\n\nI\'m now integrating the user\'s specific CSS. I\'ve taken note of the `.card`, `.card::after`, and `.card::before` styles, which define width, height, background, box-shadow, and border-radius.  I\'m also considering additional effects like a gentle wobble or organic breathing on hover, but may stick to static morphing. I\'m focusing on preserving the user\'s intended aesthetic.\n\n\n**Verifying Precise Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on ensuring pixel-perfect reproduction of the user\'s component.  The key is to strictly adhere to the white background and ensure no unintended UI elements are introduced. I am evaluating the `App.tsx` container and the CSS, specifically the `html`, `body`, and `#root` rules, and I\'m double-checking the container\'s classes. The existing CSS will be reviewed and possibly simplified. I am considering smooth physics interactions for mouse interaction as an enhancement, but only if they don\'t deviate from the core clean white aesthetic.\n\n\n**Implementing New Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the `.card` styles, and have added specifications for size, color, shadows, and rounded borders, including specific border-radius values. I\'ve also implemented transition effects on the `.card` to scale and change radius. Active state styling has been applied for drag-and-drop capabilities. I\'m also styling pseudo-elements `::after` and `::before`. The user interaction idea is in the back of my mind, and I may incorporate it later.\n\n\n**Prioritizing UI Simplicity**\n\nI\'m now honing in on the UI, and the focus is pure white with no additional UI elements. The droplet card will render at the center, as the user designed it, and I\'ll consider smooth dragging. The absolute priority is to ensure the core droplet displays perfectly in this pure white environment, and all of this must be achieved with zero HUD or UI chrome. I\'m taking a close look at the `motion` elements.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_844419337482892686"
      name: "replace_file_content"
