  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Envisioning Dynamic Water**\n\nI\'m now focusing on making this water drop truly dynamic. I\'m exploring how it can change shape when it \"lands\" at the bottom of the site and how a user might interact with it. The idea is to have drag-and-throw interactions, and a realistic recoil effect.\n\n\n**Designing Liquid Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the physics of this liquid interaction. Specifically, I\'m defining the droplet\'s behavior: gravity-driven descent, squash on impact, and free-dragging by the user. I\'m focusing on realistic recoil off screen edges, based on velocity and angle, and ensuring continuous, organic jiggle effects during movement and at rest. The white-site visual aesthetic with realistic shading will be maintained.\n\n\n**Modeling Liquid Interactions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the underlying physics model. My focus is the `x`, `y` position, and `vx`, `vy` velocity of the drop, including the gravity, air resistance, and friction. I\'m also addressing screen boundaries with bounce restitution to trigger horizontal and vertical impact squashes. I\'m prioritizing volume conservation with dynamic `w` and `h` properties to model deformations.\n\n\n**Refining Droplet Deformation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the detailed deformation mechanics. The core is impact compression: height shrinks, width expands upon impact, using variables like `verticalSquash` and `horizontalSquash`, and spring constants for a wobble. For flight, I\'m working to stretch the droplet along its velocity vector, with rotation and `speed`-dependent stretch factors. Lastly, I\'m focused on the dynamic `border-radius` morphing to accurately flatten the bottom when at rest.\n\n\n**Implementing Fluid Recoil**\n\nI\'m now integrating drag-and-throw interactions. I\'ve defined pointer events for seamless mouse and touch input. When grabbed, the droplet will now follow the pointer with a slight squish, and a rolling buffer of movements will calculate release velocity. Upon release, the droplet will now be flung with the calculated velocity, and behave based on existing physics, with dynamic wobbles and bounces. The focus is to make it ricochet off walls, with realistic fluid recoil effects.\n\n\n**Optimizing Frame Rates**\n\nI\'m now integrating the droplet\'s physics using `requestAnimationFrame` with React hooks, prioritizing silky smooth performance. I\'m focusing on directly manipulating CSS transforms for speed and also verifying highlight behavior under these transformations. I\'m also confirming that existing color and shadow effects correctly adapt to deformation and surface impact.\n\n\n\n**Defining Drop Dynamics**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on key drop properties and state variables. I\'m nailing down the center coordinates, velocity, base radius, and screen boundaries. Also, I\'m setting up state variables for dragging, wobble effects, and their spring-damper parameters. Finally, I\'m defining the bottom impact behavior with velocity-dependent squashes and realistic rebound, where I use a `restitution` to model a damping effect.\n\n\n**Refining Surface Interaction**\n\nI\'m now specifically working on the droplet\'s behavior when it settles at the bottom. I\'m focusing on simulating surface tension; I\'m calculating the \"bottom contact\" proximity to make the droplet\'s base border-radius dynamically flatten, and the top curvature. Friction is also being incorporated to simulate realistic resting behavior. I\'m implementing wall interaction behavior, including x-axis adjustments and restitution based on `restitution_wall`. I\'m using clamping techniques with `wobbleVelX` and `wobbleVelY` to model energy transfer upon collision.\n\n\n**Modeling Dynamic Shape**\n\nI\'m now making the water drop\'s shape dynamically accurate, starting with its resting state, which accounts for bottom contact; I\'m using combined scaling to account for wobbles and calculate horizontal and vertical scaling with a volume preservation formula. In-flight, I am applying a speed-dependent stretch based on velocity angle, and I will be using dynamic `border-radius` perturbation to add a more organic, fluid appearance.\n\n\n**Implementing Shape Effects**\n\nI\'m now focusing on realistic shape variations based on contact. Variables `r5`, `r6`, `r7`, and `r8` are key to this, which drive dynamic border-radius changes when the drop settles or wobbles. The goal is an organic look for resting, dragging, and rebounding, with effects like flattening and stretching. I\'m also ensuring the drop responds directly to pointer input, setting `isDragging` appropriately.\n\n\n**Implementing Drag Motion**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on drag and release interactions. I\'m recording the pointer\'s offset from the droplet\'s center to ensure the grab feels natural and track its position history for accurate throwing velocity calculations. When the pointer moves, I\'m setting a drag stretch effect, and on release, I calculate and apply the throw velocity, and trigger initial wobble based on speed.\n\n\n\n**Refining Friction Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now integrating surface friction. My thoughts focus on realistic settling; friction\'s effect on horizontal velocity is key. Specifically, I\'m now ensuring the droplet gently wobbles and comes to a complete rest, modeling surface contact and the transition to a puddle form. I\'m also ensuring a seamless visual with the drop\'s interactions with its container.\n\n\n**Evaluating Shadow Scaling**\n\nNow, I\'m verifying the shadow effect under transformations; specifically the `box-shadow` scaling. I\'m focusing on validating whether the scale transformation also looks good when the droplet is squashed or stretched. Then, I\'m also now fine-tuning the wobble physics; I\'m implementing two coupled oscillation modes: a harmonic oscillator for squash and stretch, and a liquid oscillation frequency for the bounciness.\n\n\n**Implementing Edge Collisions**\n\nI\'m now implementing edge recoil. High-speed collisions now trigger an impulse proportional to normal velocity, with rebound velocity adjusted by restitution. I\'m dynamically deforming the droplet with each impact, adjusting `wobbleVelX` and `wobbleVelY` for a fluid elastic recoil effect. I\'m also ensuring the viewport resizes are handled correctly to keep the drop within the window.\n\nI\'m also setting up the initial drop behavior. The drop will fall, rest, and await the user.\n\n\n"
