  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Deformation Limits**\n\nI\'m focusing on the user\'s observation about excessive deformation, which impacts the recoil. My approach is to limit the squash and stretch amplitudes. I aim to maintain the water drop\'s natural shape, preventing exaggerated or glitchy visuals. This should improve the perceived recoil.\n\n\n**Refining Recoil & Design**\n\nI\'m now refining the recoil\'s intensity to achieve a more subtle, liquid-like effect, reducing the pinball-like bouncing. My current approach involves clamping squash/stretch ratios within a [0.75, 1.25] range and significantly restraining wobble velocities. Additionally, I\'m exploring transforming the droplet into a 1:1 app (square window), complete with a matching taskbar element. This taskbar will adopt the same water-glass aesthetic. The goal is to make the app\'s launch appear as a smooth deformation from the taskbar icon, creating a cohesive visual experience.\n\n\n**Visualizing Organic Transitions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a new organic transition effect. When the user clicks the app, I envision the taskbar icon stretching and budding, like a liquid drop separating and transitioning into the main 1:1 app window. Upon closing, it will smoothly merge back into the taskbar. Inside the app, refined liquid physics will allow dragging, gentle throwing, and subtle recoil.\n\n\n**Exploring App Window Contents**\n\nI\'m now considering the content of the 1:1 app window. I\'m exploring minimalist, water-themed possibilities. Thinking about a droplet clock/zen timer, a fluid notes app, or a clean, minimalist workspace with droplet-based controls like a visualizer. Considering past user instructions, such as a totally white UI, this helps guide the design choices.\n\n\n**Visualizing Fluid Transitions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the aesthetic connection between the taskbar and the app. Both will share the same water material with matching glares and rounded corners. My focus is on a smooth transition: the taskbar icon morphs, stretches, and expands organically into the 1:1 app window. I\'m exploring SVG gooey filters, liquid meta-balls, or smooth spring extrusions to achieve the budding, detaching effect. The goal is a cohesive, immersive experience.\n\n\n**Implementing Liquid Integration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the seamless integration and docking back into the taskbar. Minimization and restoration are functioning. The SVG gooey liquid filter, or metaball/smooth spring morph, seems promising. With CSS/SVG, I can animate the droplet budding from the taskbar, stretching with a liquid bridge, and smoothly blooming into the app window. I\'m refining deformation and recoil parameters; extreme squishing was previously caused by excessive squashPower values, and I\'ll now address these physics issues for a more natural recoil.\n\n\n**Refining Physical Parameters**\n\nI\'ve tightened the deformation clamps; scaleX/Y now range from 0.85 to 1.18, wobbleX/Y from -0.15 to 0.15. Restitution is now 0.30-0.35, damping is increased (SPRING_DAMP: 14, SPRING_K: 90), and borderRadius perturbation is trimmed to \302\2616%. A gentle bottom dome flattening is implemented. These changes create a softer, more viscous water effect and help address previous recoil issues.\n\n\n**Designing Taskbar Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the app\'s visual style. I plan to create a transparent glass body with inset dark shadows for depth and white glares for reflection. I\'m aiming for dual droplet specular highlights, a tiny minimize/dock bead, and a matching taskbar icon. Inside the app, I envision a zen bubble clock or a droplet note app, with subtle water ripples. It\'s designed to be clean, minimalist, and harmoniously pure white.\n\n\n**Defining Taskbar Placement**\n\nI\'m now thinking about the taskbar placement, which will be docked at the bottom center. The taskbar will use the same water droplet material as the main app, with transparency, shadows, and rounded corners. It will also have subtle specular glares, much like water on a white surface. I also envision the app icon as a miniature water droplet with a gentle indicator dot when active.\n\n\n**Animating App Expansion**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the app\'s opening transition. When the user clicks the icon, a droplet buds from the taskbar, stretches upwards, and then \"snaps\" free, blooming into the 1:1 app. I\'ll use a smooth spring to achieve this liquid pinch-off, with the teardrop shape separating from the taskbar surface. The physics will transition into the interactive mode.\n\n\n\n**Defining Launch Mechanisms**\n\nI\'m now determining the app\'s initial state and launch behavior. I\'m considering starting the app docked in the taskbar or having a subtle liquid pulse that invites user interaction. Clicking the taskbar icon can trigger either launch method. I\'ll also allow toggling dock/undock, and a drag-and-drop mechanism for snapping the droplet to the taskbar.\n\nI am now focusing on the CSS classes and structure. I will define `.water-taskbar` and `.water-icon` in `src/index.css` for the taskbar. I will also make the 1:1 window have either a `.water-window` or share the water droplet style via `.card`.\n\n\n"
