  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Core Styles**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the core styles for the application\'s interface. Specifically, I\'m working on the `.card` class, which will represent the 1:1 window droplet. I\'m noting the dimensions (220px by 220px) and the aspect ratio (1:1), all per user\'s request.\n\n\n**Visualizing Liquid Transition**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the app\'s taskbar behavior. When the user clicks the icon, I envision a smooth animation where a \"liquid neck\" extends from the taskbar bead. The 1:1 window will deform as it rises, expanding vertically and compressing horizontally, simulating surface tension. The taskbar itself will be at the bottom, styled transparently with a glassy look and the specified shadows and bead features, without any dynamics.\n\n\n**Refining Liquid Detachment**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the detachment animation of the 1:1 window. The key is simulating a gentle, liquid-like separation from the taskbar. I\'m focusing on the deformation and recoil. To make the liquid motion feel more natural, I\'m significantly damping the deformation using clamped scales and lower recoil restitution. Gentle impact squash is being implemented to keep a more natural feel. The border radius variation will be kept in a strict range to avoid jagged angles, resulting in a super smooth, soft, gelatinous water-like wobble.\n\n\n**Visualizing App Detachment**\n\nI\'m now detailing the full detachment animation. I envision the app icon as a resting liquid bead when docked. Upon click, it transitions to `isExtruding: true`. A liquid bridge or metaball will render between the taskbar icon and a droplet starting at scale `0.22`. Over 0.6s, the droplet stretches vertically (`scaleY: 1.45, scaleX: 0.7`) with a fluid neck. This neck then thins and snaps, the droplet floats to the center expanding from 48px to the full 220px 1:1 window with an elastic ripple.\n\n\n**Simulating Docking Animation**\n\nI\'m now detailing the docking transition. I envision a smooth arc motion back toward the taskbar icon, elongating downwards (`scaleY: 1.3, scaleX: 0.75`) as it nears. The goal is to merge it into the taskbar bead with a gentle ripple effect. I\'m starting to sketch out the CSS in `src/index.css` for both the water droplet, the 1:1 window, and the water taskbar. I am also checking the ideal specular glares the taskbar needs.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Taskbar Styling**\n\nI\'m now detailing the CSS for the taskbar components. I\'ve defined the `.water-taskbar::after` element for the subtle highlights and the `.taskbar-bead` (app icon) with its specified dimensions, shadows, and hover effect. The `.taskbar-bead` styling includes a custom border-radius for the liquid bead appearance and a `::before` element for the specular glare. I\'ve also incorporated a basic hover transform.\n\n\n**Defining Window Contents**\n\nI\'m now detailing the contents of the 1:1 window. I\'m focusing on a clean, minimalist aesthetic. I plan to incorporate a minimize bead in the top-right corner. The window will feature interactive liquid ripples upon tapping, and a minimalist time / zen breath pulse that complements the liquid theme. The background will be pure white, keeping it uncluttered. I\'m also considering the liquid neck for the exit animation.\n\n\n"
