    thinking: "**Analyzing HTML Integration**\n\nI\'m currently focused on integrating the inline script into `index.html`. My approach involves placing the script within the `<head>` section. I\'m considering using an Immediately Invoked Function Expression (IIFE) to encapsulate the functionality and protect the global scope. This initial script will attempt to intercept and modify the `fetch` function.\n\n\n**Refining Fetch Override**\n\nI\'ve been examining how the extension\'s script interacts with `window.fetch`. I\'ve realized that the extension attempts a direct assignment, which fails because the original `fetch` is a getter-only property in strict mode. My code introduces a setter, allowing interception. Now, I\'m thinking about the implications of having both a getter and a setter on `window.fetch`. This approach seems more robust, but needs careful handling.\n\n\n**Confirming Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that calls to `fetch` continue to function as intended, even after the attempted override. An error was correctly thrown during the operation. I\'m also now implementing an `error` event listener to suppress extension-related errors. This should help prevent those errors from disrupting the application. I will look for errors that originate from the `chrome-extension` namespace and try to suppress them.\n\n\n"
