  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Request Details**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s request. My focus is on thoroughly understanding the nuances of multi-window state management and the interactions between these \"droplet\" windows. I need to pinpoint the essential requirements for independent spawning, merging, and bounce behavior. I\'m prioritizing a clean, refactored approach.\n\n\n**Designing the Architecture**\n\nI\'ve moved on to designing the architecture. I\'m building out the data structures for multiple independent windows, envisioning a `droplets: DropletData[]` array. Each droplet will have an ID, position, velocity, and properties for the liquid wobble effect and scale. I\'m also including a status field for each window, with states like \'extruding\' and \'merging\'.\n\n\n**Defining Droplet Properties**\n\nI\'m now nailing down the precise properties for each droplet window. I\'ve got `dragPointerId`, `dragOffsetX`, and related drag state properties, and I need a mechanism for displaying an index, clock, or content title like \"Droplet #1\". I\'m considering using either a DOM reference or a ref map.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m also considering the implications of the taskbar spawning behavior. I\'m focusing on the animation details, like the \"budding\" and \"extruding\" effect, with the liquid bridge. The launch and neck snap sounds are critical, as is controlling the maximum simultaneous droplet count. I\'m thinking about the animated return to the taskbar upon closing, with the recorded merge sound.\n\n\n**Determining Collision Outcomes**\n\nNow I\'m diving into the collision logic: deciding between bounce or merge. Gentle collisions, low relative velocity, and surface tension coalesce two droplets into one. The new droplet\'s volume, radius, position, and momentum are easily calculated. I\'m focusing on the visual and auditory cues: a merge sound with a jiggle effect for an authentic result.\n\n\n**Calculating Bounce Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the bounce mechanics for high-velocity collisions. I\'ve worked out the condition for impact ($d < R_1 + R_2$), the normal collision response, and the inelastic impulse calculation. I\'m focusing on the deformation: the droplets\' squash and bulge effects. The impact sound, driven by `v_rel`, is critical. I\'m also ensuring the user can easily interpret bounce vs. merge rules.\n\n\n**Refining Collision Rules**\n\nI\'m now refining the collision rules, specifically the bounce vs. merge threshold. A higher relative speed, above 300 px/s, will cause a bounce, complete with liquid recoil. Gentle collisions, or directly dragging onto another, will merge, with a satisfying coalesce. Users can also always spawn more droplets via the taskbar. Merged droplets get an updated badge count, slightly larger size, and a deeper bounce sound. Each window will have its own minimize/dock button.\n\n\n**Updating State Structure**\n\nI\'ve significantly refactored the state management. The `stateRef` now incorporates a richer `DropletItem` interface to handle each window\'s properties, including position, velocity, and wobble effects. I\'m also tracking drag interaction and lifecycle events, with a `status` field for different states, to allow transitions for animations.\n\n\n**Implementing the RAF Loop**\n\nI\'m now implementing the request animation frame (RAF) loop. I\'ve focused on ensuring 60-120fps physics without excessive React re-renders. I\'m storing droplet DOM elements in `cardElementsRef`. Within the RAF loop, I\'ll update each droplet\'s status and run droplet-to-droplet collision detection. I\'m calculating the distance and relative velocity for collision logic.\n\n\n**Finalizing Collision Physics**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the collision response, focusing specifically on the velocity threshold. I\'ve set the hard fling threshold to 280 px/s. Above this, the droplets bounce with momentum exchange and perpendicular squashing, triggering the wall impact sound. Otherwise, it merges; droplet I absorbs droplet J. The merge process combines masses and velocities, and sets a target scale based on the combined volume.\n\n\n**Verifying the Architecture**\n\nI\'m now running through a final verification of the architecture and implementation details. My focus is on confirmation of the state update frequency: it is only done on infrequent lifecycle events, ensuring the animation frame loop is fast and smooth. I\'m confirming there are no React re-renders in the physics step, and zero GC pressure. I\'m also confirming the taskbar UI components are correct and functional.\n\n\n**Evaluating Visual Refinements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual polish. I\'m considering the liquid bridge rendering, leaning towards a multi-path SVG approach for efficiency. I\'m also confirming the content within each droplet window, with index, live clock, merge indicators, and minimize/close behaviors. The interactive ripple effect upon tapping is also a key consideration. Finally, I\'m testing the bounce math to ensure it is accurate.\n\n\n**Calculating Collision Impact**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the nitty-gritty details of the collision impact. I\'m focusing on the exact rigid/elastic circle collision resolution, including mass weighting, overlap correction and relative velocity. I\'m carefully calculating the normal vector, overlap and impulse, and the restitution factor. The goal is accurate, visual deformation in response to impacts. I\'m also ensuring the droplets\' wobble effects are properly updated.\n\n\n**Verifying Coalescence Physics**\n\nI\'m now verifying the merge logic, which is triggered when relative speed is below 280 px/s or during direct dragging. I\'m focusing on the calculations: mass and velocity combination, and coordinate averages. I am also checking the target scale, driven by combined volume, and the surface tension effects of fluid agitation. I\'m confirming that the `dropJ.status` gets updated after merging.\n\n\n"
