  planner_response {
    response: "I have refactored the state management to support multiple independent droplet windows with inter-droplet collision physics:\n\n- **Multi-Droplet State Architecture**:\n  - Refactored the physics engine to maintain an active registry of independent droplet windows (`dropletsRef` and `dropletList`).\n  - **Spawning from Taskbar**: Every click on the taskbar bead extrudes a new independent 1:1 droplet app window (labeled `Droplet #1`, `Droplet #2`, etc.) with its own physics state, live clock, and interactive ripples.\n  - **Individual Docking**: Each droplet window can be closed/docked back into the taskbar via its minimize bead or by dragging it close to the dock.\n  - **Taskbar Badge**: The taskbar displays an active liquid indicator dot with the live count of active windows.\n\n- **Collision Logic: Bounce vs. Merge**:\n  - **Elastic Bounce (High Velocity)**: When droplets are thrown or collide at high speeds (`> 280 px/s`), they bounce off each other with momentum exchange, contact-normal squashing, and real recorded impact sounds.\n  - **Coalescence / Merge (Low Velocity / Dragged)**: When droplets touch gently or when one is dragged into another, surface tension merges them into a single, larger droplet with combined mass, expanded scale, fluid shockwave agitation, a merge multiplier badge (`\302\267 \303\2272`, `\302\267 \303\2273`), and real recorded merge acoustics.\n  - **Merge All Action**: Clicking the secondary bead on the taskbar pulls all floating droplets together to coalesce into a master droplet."
    thinking: "**Reviewing Requirements Now**\n\nI\'m currently reviewing the user\'s requirements. Specifically, I\'ve refactored the state architecture to handle dynamic creation, focusing on spawning multiple independent droplet windows from the taskbar, which I think is a key focus for this project.\n\n\n**Implementing Fluid Mechanics**\n\nI\'ve been working on the fluid mechanics aspects. High-velocity collisions result in elastic bounces with momentum exchange and impact sounds. Low-velocity interactions trigger a merge, coalescing into a larger droplet with combined mass, conserving surface area using the provided mathematical principles.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have refactored the state management to support multiple independent droplet windows with inter-droplet collision physics:\n\n- **Multi-Droplet State Architecture**:\n  - Refactored the physics engine to maintain an active registry of independent droplet windows (`dropletsRef` and `dropletList`).\n  - **Spawning from Taskbar**: Every click on the taskbar bead extrudes a new independent 1:1 droplet app window (labeled `Droplet #1`, `Droplet #2`, etc.) with its own physics state, live clock, and interactive ripples.\n  - **Individual Docking**: Each droplet window can be closed/docked back into the taskbar via its minimize bead or by dragging it close to the dock.\n  - **Taskbar Badge**: The taskbar displays an active liquid indicator dot with the live count of active windows.\n\n- **Collision Logic: Bounce vs. Merge**:\n  - **Elastic Bounce (High Velocity)**: When droplets are thrown or collide at high speeds (`> 280 px/s`), they bounce off each other with momentum exchange, contact-normal squashing, and real recorded impact sounds.\n  - **Coalescence / Merge (Low Velocity / Dragged)**: When droplets touch gently or when one is dragged into another, surface tension merges them into a single, larger droplet with combined mass, expanded scale, fluid shockwave agitation, a merge multiplier badge (`\302\267 \303\2272`, `\302\267 \303\2273`), and real recorded merge acoustics.\n  - **Merge All Action**: Clicking the secondary bead on the taskbar pulls all floating droplets together to coalesce into a master droplet."
