  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Vapor Trails**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the implementation of a \"vapor trail\" effect for high-speed droplets. I plan to use a series of shrinking, fading, semi-transparent SVG circles to trace the droplet\'s path. I\'m exploring the best way to handle the animation and rendering for smooth visuals at varying speeds.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Representation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the visual styling of the vapor trail. I\'m leaning toward using translucent, pale blue circles with radial gradients or soft blurs to create a water-mist effect. The plan is to have the circles shrink and fade over time, with their radii and opacity linked to their age. I\'m aiming for a dynamic, ephemeral appearance.\n\n\n**Optimizing Vapor Trail Rendering**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into performance optimization for the vapor trail. The application runs a 60-120fps RAF loop and uses an SVG overlay. I\'m exploring the most efficient method to render the trails within this environment, aiming to minimize re-renders. Options include maintaining a `vaporParticlesRef` in memory or directly manipulating SVG DOM elements via a `<g ref={vaporGroupRef}>`, updating the particles within the RAF loop. I\'m prioritizing minimal overhead to ensure smooth performance. I also want to experiment with the slight dispersion.\n\n\n**Implementing Particle Pool**\n\nI\'ve decided to implement a particle pool to manage the vapor trail\'s circles efficiently. I\'m thinking of a maximum of 60 circles within a `<g ref={vaporGroupRef}>`. Each `<circle>` element will be created once and then reused, eliminating garbage collection overhead. I\'ll spawn particles every 25ms, if the velocity exceeds 360, and calculate their initial radius and opacity based on the drop\'s scale. The existing beads\' age will be taken into account for opacity and radius.\n\n\n**Defining Vapor Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now refining the visual style of the vapor beads. I\'m exploring gradients and blur effects to create a water-mist appearance. Specifically, I\'m considering using radial gradients or `rgba` fills with subtle strokes and SVG filters for a soft glow, to simulate an authentic condensation effect. The goal is a dynamically shrinking and fading vapor trail, triggered above a speed threshold.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_16929095587434937938"
