  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing SVG Opacity**\n\nI\'ve been looking closely at how the SVG\'s opacity is being managed, specifically focusing on the conditional rendering tied to `bridgePaths.length`. It looks like the opacity is initially set to 0, which makes sense. I now suspect the `bridgeSvgRef` and its potential updates will be the critical element. The presence or absence of this may be the key to triggering the visibility change, so I will now focus on that ref.\n\n\n**Designing Vapor Trails**\n\nI\'ve shifted focus to designing the vapor trail system. I am considering a dedicated SVG layer with a `VaporParticle` interface to manage individual particles with properties like position, velocity, radius, and life. The goal is to make the trails always visible and gracefully stream behind the droplet. The plan is to spawn particles in the RAF loop based on droplet speed for floating droplets.\n\n\n**Implementing Vapor Emitters**\n\nI\'m now implementing the vapor emitter logic and considering a speed threshold of 350 for particle generation. I\'m focusing on the timing mechanism using timestamps to avoid excessive particle emissions, with emissions occurring every ~20ms, which means every 2 frames. Initial particle radius is being calculated based on droplet scale and velocity. I\'ve got the offset of the particles from the droplet to be based on speed and random variance as well. Currently, I\'m aiming for a maximum of approximately 70 active vapor particles. I will be updating these in the RAF loop.\n\n\n**Refining Vapor Particles**\n\nI\'m now refining the vapor particle behavior and rendering. Each particle drifts due to inertia and air drag, shrinking and fading as its life progresses. I calculate particle position, velocity, radius, and opacity updates in each frame. I have optimized rendering using a fixed pool of SVG `<circle>` elements, updating attributes like `cx`, `cy`, `r`, and `opacity` in the RAF loop. Inactive particles are hidden, maintaining performance.\n\n\n**Optimizing Vapor Aesthetics**\n\nI\'ve been working on the visual style of the vapor circles. I\'m focusing on creating an aesthetic that mimics water vapor condensation. I plan to use a radial gradient for the fill with a subtle soft stroke and a Gaussian blur filter to make each circle appear as a tiny, glistening mist droplet. I also plan to make the droplets shrink and fade as though they are evaporating into nothingness. I want to execute everything in less than 0.2ms per frame. I want ZERO DOM allocations and ZERO garbage collection.\n\n\n**Evaluating Vapor Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the dynamic behavior and integration of the vapor trail within the application. I need to make sure the vapor trail accurately reflects the droplet\'s physics, including the curved paths and wall bounces. I\'m focusing on seamlessly integrating the trail\'s behavior with the droplet\'s speed, ensuring it appears only when the speed exceeds the threshold. I am inspecting where I can add `lastVaporTime` to `DropletState`, to manage the timing, and I need to check the pool size of `MAX_VAPOR = 75`. I will also use `vaporParticlesRef`, `vaporGroupRef` and `vaporCircleRefs`. I want to make sure the vapor trail layer is behind all other windows.\n\n\n**Reviewing SVG Declarations**\n\nI\'m now reviewing the SVG declarations, specifically the pre-rendered `<circle>` elements and the use of `vaporCircleRefs.current` for O(1) access. I see a clean and declarative structure. The radial gradient and blur filter are well-defined. This approach seems optimal for performance, using a fixed pool of SVG circles. The current style is `display:none` and is the key to managing visibility. This is a very clean solution for achieving the effect I\'m targeting, and it should be very performant as well.\n\n\n**Verifying Vapor Physics**\n\nI\'m now verifying the vapor physics updates and emission mechanics, focusing on the RAF loop processing. I\'m confirming I iterate through active particles, and update their `cx`, `cy`, `r`, and `opacity`. I\'m confirming that the display of non-active circles is correctly set to `none`. I\'m reviewing the emission logic, including the calculation of the emission interval based on speed, and the initial radius, opacity, and velocity of each particle. Also I\'m checking the lifetime updates: the radius and opacity calculations, to ensure they\'re functioning correctly.\n\n\n"
