  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Animation Request**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s animation request, specifically the \'fade to nothing\' effect for droplets at a miniscule scale to simulate evaporation. My primary focus is identifying instances where droplets reach this small scale and applying the appropriate animation. One key area is the \'docking\' transition, where a droplet moves to the taskbar.\n\n\n**Defining Evaporation Animation**\n\nI\'m now designing the \'evaporating\' state and its associated animation for the droplets. The plan includes a new status: `\'evaporating\'` and its corresponding properties. I\'m focusing on `evaporateProgress`, `evaporateStartTime`, `evaporateDuration`, `evaporateStartX`, and `evaporateStartY`, aiming for a visual fade-out effect. The timing will range between 0.35 and 0.45 seconds. This will improve realism and be applied during docking, merging, and shrinking below a threshold.\n\n\n**Refining Evaporation Effects**\n\nI\'m now refining the visual details of the evaporation animation. The scale shrinks smoothly to zero using a decelerating exponent. Simultaneously, opacity fades out more gently. The wobble effect will flatten as the droplet diminishes. I\'m excited about adding vapor particles, puffing upwards as the droplet evaporates to create the illusion of vapor. I\'m also planning subtle acoustic feedback, like a soft hiss, to complete the effect. Importantly, evaporation is triggered during droplet merges.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the evaporation sequence into various droplet interactions. The new `evaporating` status will be key. During docking into the taskbar, droplets now smoothly fade into the liquid. Droplets smaller than `0.22` scale automatically evaporate with the visual effects. I\'m investigating how to enable user-triggered evaporation, such as through double-taps or flings, or an explicit \"Evaporate\" option. I\'m currently looking into the `src/App.tsx` file in the RAF loop to figure out how to add the code for this effect.\n\n\n**Optimizing Opacity Control**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the opacity control for droplets, particularly within the `.card` element. I\'ve noted that `el.style.opacity` was not dynamically managed and am planning to add it. This will ensure smooth transitions during all states. During the \'evaporating\' status, opacity will smoothly decrease, synchronized with the scale shrinking to zero. I\'m also planning to mark the status to \'merged_out\' and remove it from the droplet list once the progress reaches `1`. I am now making adjustments, so that the animations run in sync with one another.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Physics**\n\nI\'m now incorporating physical properties into the evaporation sequence. I\'ve defined equations for scale and opacity that control the smooth fade-out effect. I\'m also adding a gentle upward thermal drift for the droplets, and I\'m adding small vapor particles as visual feedback. I\'m focusing on the first few frames to ensure the particles are emitted, and synchronizing these physical parameters within the update loop. I\'m also ensuring the droplets have a status of \'merged_out\' when the transition is finished.\n\n\n**Refining Merge Transitions**\n\nI\'m now making refinements to the merge animation. When droplets merge, instead of an immediate removal, I\'m now setting the absorbed droplet\'s status to \'evaporating\'. I\'m also capturing the start time, duration, and scale for this new state. There is also a visual shrink, fade, and a delicate mist puff, along with a drift to the dominant droplet, which really looks amazing.\n\n\n"
