    thinking: "**Revising State Transition**\n\nI\'m currently rethinking the status transition in lines 536-537. Rather than setting `absorbed.status` to `\'merged_out\'` immediately, I\'m proposing an `\'evaporating\'` state. This will allow for a more nuanced and potentially smoother handling of the merging process, allowing for more steps in the future.\n\n\n**Refining Evaporation Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the visual \'fade to nothing\' animation, simulating evaporation. I\'ve introduced `evaporateStartTime`, `evaporateDuration`, and `evaporateStartScale` properties to the `absorbed` droplet. I\'m also implementing a new state, `evaporating`, triggered when a droplet\'s scale drops below 0.28. This will allow for the animated visual transition, which is guided towards the center of the dominant droplet. The logic now includes a smooth shrinking effect and the generation of mist particles.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Loop**\n\nI\'ve just added the necessary logic within the main loop to handle droplets in the `\'evaporating\'` state. This includes calculating elapsed time, determining the progress of the evaporation, and using this to smoothly shrink the droplet\'s scale and decrease its opacity, simulating the fading effect. I\'ve also added subtle drift and buoyant upward movement, plus a gradual velocity decay to make it look realistic.\n\n\n**Adding Vapor Emission**\n\nI\'ve incorporated vapor particle generation during evaporation to enhance realism. I\'m focusing on adding delicate wisps of vapor that subtly rise with gentle upward movement, driven by heat and fluid agitation. These vapor particles are emitted at a defined rate and I am adding subtle velocity decay to their upwards movement to ensure a realistic look.\n\n\n"
