  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Evaporation Animation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"fade to nothing\" animation implementation for evaporating droplets. The focus is on achieving a smooth transition as the droplet reaches a minimal scale, avoiding a harsh disappearance. I\'m exploring various animation techniques to simulate evaporation effectively, considering both visual fidelity and performance.\n\n\n**Refining Evaporation Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now implementing the evaporation animation across various scenarios. When a droplet docks, merges, or reaches a critical scale threshold, instead of immediate removal, it will transition to an \"evaporating\" state. I\'m focusing on smooth visual transitions, incorporating shrinking scales, fading opacity, and subtle micro-vapor particle effects for a convincing evaporation effect.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Logic**\n\nI\'ve got the evaporation animation logic integrated now. Upon activation, the function calculates elapsed time and progress to manage scale and opacity changes. The scale diminishes non-linearly, and opacity fades simultaneously, simulating the visual disappearance. I\'m applying thermal drift, residual horizontal velocity, and adding tiny mist particles for added realism. When evaporation is complete, the droplet status is updated for removal.\n\n\n**Adjusting Droplet Visuals**\n\nI\'m making a critical adjustment to how evaporating droplets are rendered. I\'ve realized that the current scaling clamp prevents droplets from fully disappearing visually during the evaporation process. Specifically, when the droplet\'s status is \'evaporating,\' I need to modify the scaling calculations to allow them to shrink all the way down to zero or near-zero scale, ensuring a smooth transition to nothing. I\'m also adding `opacity` and `pointerEvents` styling to the RAF loop.\n\n\n"
