    thinking: "**Analyzing Animation Requirements**\n\nI\'ve confirmed `metadata.json` and `index.html` are perfectly aligned, which is a great relief. The current focus is a deep dive into the animation requirements specifically for the \"fade to nothing\" effect when a droplet shrinks to an infinitesimal scale. I must pinpoint the criteria to make a clean implementation.\n\n\n**Defining Evaporation Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now detailing the mechanics of simulated evaporation. Droplet scale is crucial: under 0.28, evaporation begins. I have outlined three key scenarios: docking (seamless integration with the taskbar), inter-droplet collisions (fusing into the larger droplet), and user interactions (clicks, drags, and splits). These now require animation logic for smooth transitions.\n\n\n**Designing Evaporation Animation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the specific animation for when a droplet evaporates. I\'ve broken down the steps, including time calculations and scale/opacity curves for a smooth fade out. The upward drift and deceleration are incorporated to simulate vapor movement. Now, the details require the implementation of gentle mist rise and the x-axis drift. I\'m aiming for a subtle, natural-looking effect.\n\n\n**Implementing Vapor Particles**\n\nI\'ve been working on refining the vapor particle emissions during evaporation. I\'ve integrated code to spawn tiny, soft particles that gently drift upwards and disperse as the droplet shrinks, and implemented a conditional based on the \'progress\' variable to ensure the effect looks natural. I\'ve also established criteria for when the droplet should be marked as completely merged and scheduled for removal.\n\n\n**Refining Opacity and Scaling**\n\nI\'ve been revising the scaling and opacity logic, and just updated the constraints for evaporating droplets to ensure they can now shrink down to a scale of 0.001 instead of getting stuck at 0.18. I\'ve also implemented the opacity changes and pointer events to smoothly fade out the droplets during evaporation. Finally, I\'ve confirmed that the fade-out effect will naturally apply to the `.card::before` and `.card::after` elements as well. Now I\'m checking the docking transition code, particularly the lines 318-350, to confirm that it has a smooth transition.\n\n\n**Evaluating Docking Transition**\n\nI\'m now deeply involved in the docking transition. The code uses `easeIn` for smooth movement, with adjustments for scale, x/y position, and wobble effects. I\'ve pinpointed specific calculations for progress and duration, but I\'m checking how the `bridgePaths` array gets populated to draw the transition path as a \'bridge\' during docking. Specifically, I am concerned about how the transition looks from the user\'s perspective.\n\n\n\n**Refining Evaporation States**\n\nMy focus has shifted to refining the transition from merged to evaporation states. I\'ve adjusted the code to change `drop.status` to \'evaporating\' with a defined start time and duration when `progress >= 1` during docking. This change allows a more gradual transition. I\'ve also integrated similar logic within inter-droplet merges, which now triggers an evaporation state for the absorbed droplet to blend smoothly into the dominant one, enhancing the visual effect.\n\n\n**Implementing Direct Evaporation**\n\nI\'m now implementing direct evaporation triggered by user action, like a context menu. I\'ve integrated `handleEvaporate(id)` to change the status, trigger animation, and even play a ripple sound. I have implemented a conditional to check for evaporation when a droplet in floating mode reaches a very small scale, too. Now I\'m considering the evaporation start scale.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Triggers**\n\nI\'ve added multiple evaporation triggers. Now, I have integrated code to handle direct evaporation through a button or tooltip on minimize actions. Furthermore, droplets automatically evaporate when reaching a minimal scale, during merges, and through right-click actions. I\'m focusing on the animation triggers and making the transitions smooth. I\'m checking lines 318 to 355 of `/src/App.tsx` and inspecting how docking and floating transitions happen.\n\n\n**Analyzing Evaporation Transitions**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `extruding`, `docking`, and `evaporating` states. The code in the loop handles these transitions. The `extruding` state is straightforward. The `docking` state now changes to `evaporating` when the progress is complete, initiating the fade. The evaporation transition features exponential scale decay and smooth opacity adjustments. I am fine-tuning the `evaporateDuration` for an optimal visual effect.\n\n\n**Refining Mist Particle Ejection**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the mist particle ejection mechanics during droplet evaporation. I\'ve adjusted the code to introduce a subtle x-axis drift and a more gradual thermal ascent for a natural visual effect. I\'ve also integrated a probabilistic element for particle emission, considering the \'progress\' variable, to make sure the mist is visible and smooth. I\'m now testing the updated mechanics.\n\n\n**Verifying Inter-Droplet Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now checking the collision detection loop for evaporation. I\'ve confirmed that a droplet switching to \'evaporating\' status won\'t be caught in future frames, so it will smoothly complete its evaporation animation without further collisions. The code prevents repetitive interactions during the 0.32-second fade-out. I\'m satisfied the transition is clean.\n\n\n**Confirming Evaporation Transition**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed that the `pendingRemovals.push(absorbed.id)` function is correctly triggered after the absorbed droplet completes its evaporation animation. This ensures a smooth visual dissolution without premature removal, improving the user experience. I have also examined lines 630-675 to verify how the DOM styling gets updated with each active droplet window.\n\n\n"
