  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Adding New Tool Options**\n\nI\'m considering a new addition to the taskbar, a \"Surface Tension\" bead. This would be the third tool, placed alongside the existing \"Spawn bead\" and \"Merge All bead\". It would be visually distinct from the refractive tools. I think `taskbar-bead-surface-tension` is a good name for this tool.\n\n\n**Designing The Interface**\n\nI\'ve been working on the implementation of the \"Surface Tension\" bead. I\'ve got a state variable, and a ref to store its value. I\'m focusing on the visual design now. I\'m thinking of two micro-droplets in the bead, pulsing when enabled. When active, it will have a liquid tension glow with highlight glares. If disabled, it\'ll be muted. The active dot is a transparent dot, when not active.\n\n\n**Calculating Attraction Forces**\n\nI\'ve been examining the physics behind surface tension, specifically the attraction between water droplets on a hydrophobic surface. I\'m focusing on how to translate the Cheerios effect into a force calculation. I\'m looking at factors like droplet radii, contact distance, and attraction range. I\'m developing a proximity factor that smoothly increases as droplets get closer. This factor will influence the magnitude of the attractive force I\'ll be calculating.\n\n\n**Defining Force Interactions**\n\nI\'m now calculating the attractive forces between the droplets, using a proximity factor to smoothly increase attraction. The surface tension force is roughly 420px/s\302\262, and I\'m using a power of 1.4 for the factor\'s influence. I\'m also implementing velocity updates for the droplets based on their masses. Finally, I\'m working on the meniscus effect. I\'m stretching the droplet shapes along the connecting axis, creating a more realistic look as they get close.\n\n\n"
