  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Bead Implementation**\n\nOkay, so I\'m evaluating the implementation of a taskbar bead. I\'ve determined the bead size is 36x36px. Now, I\'m trying to decide if we should use a toggle class for state changes, `.taskbar-bead-toggle`, or if we can repurpose `.taskbar-bead-secondary` and add an active state. I\'m leaning toward either `.taskbar-bead-secondary.is-active` or a dedicated class for clarity.\n\n\n**Designing Surface Tension Bead**\n\nI\'m now designing the Surface Tension bead for the taskbar, aiming for a placement near the Merge All bead. I\'ve sketched out the initial HTML structure for it. I\'ve created a button with `aria-label`, `aria-pressed`, and a class based on the `surfaceTension` state, using `taskbar-bead-secondary` with `is-active` to visually indicate the toggle state.\n\n\n**Refining Toggle Visuals**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the visual representation of the toggle state. I\'m leveraging `aria-pressed` for accessibility and a class based on the `surfaceTension` state. I\'m using `taskbar-bead-secondary` with `is-active` to denote the toggle visually. I\'m exploring transitions for a smooth user experience. I\'m paying attention to the micro-indicator dot as a secondary visual cue.\n\n\n**Visualizing Tension Field**\n\nI\'m now implementing the visual representation for the surface tension toggle. I\'ve focused on the visual cues: connected droplets with a liquid bridge when active, and an illuminated indicator dot. Clicking the bead toggles the `surfaceTension` state, triggering sound, and instantly updating a ref. A liquid wobble will be applied to any floating droplets, acting as a visual cue when tension activates or deactivates.\n\n\n**Calculating Attraction Range**\n\nI\'m now diving into the physics behind the surface tension attraction. I\'ve defined the attraction range based on droplet radii and a scaling factor. I\'m focusing on the capillary attraction force, calculating a proximity value and a tension force that increases non-linearly as droplets get closer. I\'m carefully considering how to apply this mutual pull force to the free-floating droplets within the simulation.\n\n\n**Implementing Attraction Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now implementing the attraction forces between droplets. I\'m applying the calculated tension force as a mutual pull, adjusting velocity components based on mass and time step. I\'m also adding a viscoelastic meniscus stretch effect, simulating the liquid bridge\'s behavior. I am focusing on what happens when the distance between droplets dips below the minimum attraction distance. And, I\'m working out the conditions that trigger a merge.\n\n\n**Verifying Coalescence Behavior**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that droplets now coalesce seamlessly using `surfaceTensionRef.current`, with motion mimicking real-world behavior, whether resting or floating. I\'m checking the interaction between floor friction and droplet movement to refine the experience, specifically considering the scenario where droplets might be at rest, or not. I\'ve located the relevant collision code for floor interaction in `src/App.tsx` and am analyzing lines 380-405, and am planning to determine if I need to make a correction for zero horizontal velocity.\n\n\n**Evaluating Default State**\n\nI\'m now considering the initial state of surface tension. I think it should be enabled by default. This will allow the user to immediately experience the realistic water behavior. I plan to provide clear visual feedback and make the toggle easily accessible on the taskbar so that users may switch between independent bouncing.\n\n\n"
