  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Attraction Dynamics**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the behavior of the attraction field. Specifically, I\'m examining the scenario where `surfaceTensionRef.current` is true. My attention is drawn to the conditions where the distance (`dist`) between two drops falls within the `minDist` and `attractRange` thresholds. I\'m noting how the radius of each drop (`rA`, `rB`) is calculated based on their base radius and scale. This is where the core of the attraction calculations seem to reside.\n\n\n**Investigating Proximity Impacts**\n\nI\'m now diving into the calculations for `minDist` and `attractRange`, and how they relate to droplet radii. I\'m especially interested in the `surfaceTensionRef.current` condition, which triggers the attraction field. I\'m focusing on the `proximity` calculation that determines `tensionStrength`, and I see the impact of `proximity` on the wobble of the drops, which is directly impacted by the distance between the drops.\n\n\n**Examining Merge Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now investigating the merge conditions when drops get close. I see that `surfaceTensionRef.current` plays a crucial role.  If true, they drift together and stretch. If not, they bounce at high speeds.  I\'m focusing on the `shouldMerge` boolean, which considers surface tension, relative speed, and dragging states. I\'m noting the specific thresholds for speed and distance that influence merging.\n\n\n**Exploring Merge Refinement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the merge process for a smoother visual transition. My current thought is to incorporate the water merge sound and fluid jiggle on the merging droplet. I\'m aiming for a seamless integration when droplets contact, creating a single larger droplet.\n\n\n**Analyzing UI Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now shifting focus to the user interface, specifically, the liquid indicator dot. I\'m examining the conditional styling applied based on `activeCount`, noting the transitions and opacity adjustments. I\'m observing the behavior and appearance of the active count display within the dot. I\'m also considering the implications of the surface tension toggle button and its accessibility implications.\n\n\n**Evaluating Surface Tension Display**\n\nI\'m now examining the visual representation of surface tension. I\'m focusing on the toggle button and its associated styling. I see the importance of the visual feedback provided by the two coalescing water droplet beads. I\'m noting the changes to size, color, and opacity of the dots based on the state of `surfaceTension`. I\'m also considering the text displayed in the `title` attribute for accessibility.\n\n\n**Assessing User Interface Styling**\n\nI\'m now closely examining the styling of the surface tension toggle, noting the transition durations and color variations. The sizing and opacity changes are of particular interest, especially the visual feedback of coalescing water droplet beads. I\'m focusing on how the UI reacts and provides cues, and the accessibility implications of the `title` attribute. Also, the liquid indicator dot.\n\n\n**Refining Toggle Feedback**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential visual enhancements for the surface tension toggle. The existing ripple sound provides audio feedback, and the connecting beads/illuminated dot offer visual cues. I\'m exploring subtle liquid ripple effects for the taskbar as additional feedback during toggling, but I think that the current implementation is very good. I\'m reviewing all references to `surfaceTension` and `surfaceTensionRef` to ensure consistency.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_4658160790576599008"
      name: "replace_file_content"
      arguments_json: "{\"AllowMultiple\":false,\"Description\":\"Add surfaceTension state and toggle callback with audio and droplet agitation feedback\",\"EndLine\":130,\"Instruction\":\"Add surfaceTension state and ref and toggleSurfaceTension handler\",\"ReplacementContent\":\"  // Dynamic list of active droplet windows\\n  const [dropletList, setDropletList] = useState<{ id: string; index: number; merges: number }[]>([]);\\n  const [currentTime, setCurrentTime] = useState(\'\');\\n  const [ripples, setRipples] = useState<WindowRipple[]>([]);\\n\\n  // Surface tension toggle state & ref for 60-120fps RAF physics loop\\n  const [surfaceTension, setSurfaceTension] = useState(true);\\n  const surfaceTensionRef = useRef(true);\\n\\n  // Toggle surface tension mutual attraction between droplets\\n  const toggleSurfaceTension = useCallback(() => {\\n    initAudioOnGesture();\\n    playRippleSound();\\n    setSurfaceTension((prev) => {\\n      const next = !prev;\\n      surfaceTensionRef.current = next;\\n\\n      // Agitate floating droplets slightly when tension field changes\\n      for (const d of dropletsRef.current) {\\n        if (d.status === \'floating\') {\\n          d.wobbleVelX += (Math.random() - 0.5) * 5;\\n          d.wobbleVelY += (Math.random() - 0.5) * 5;\\n        }\\n      }\\n      return next;\\n    });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  // Simulation physics state in refs (60-120fps direct RAF updates)\",\"StartLine\":107,\"TargetContent\":\"  // Dynamic list of active droplet windows\\n  const [dropletList, setDropletList] = useState<{ id: string; index: number; merges: number }[]>([]);\\n  const [currentTime, setCurrentTime] = useState(\'\');\\n  const [ripples, setRipples] = useState<WindowRipple[]>([]);\\n\\n  // Simulation physics state in refs (60-120fps direct RAF updates)\",\"TargetFile\":\"/src/App.tsx\",\"toolAction\":\"Adding surface tension state and toggle function\",\"toolSummary\":\"Add surfaceTension state to App.tsx\"}"
