  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Adding Splash Particles**\n\nI\'ve decided to incorporate a specialized SVG group or layer, specifically for \"Splash Particles\" within the SVG layer. I\'m now examining the styling options for the individual splash droplets, considering both gradients and fills. Currently, I\'m leaning toward a radial gradient.\n\n\n**Refining Droplet Appearance**\n\nI\'ve crafted a radial gradient and drop shadow to simulate realistic water droplets. Implementing `fill=\"url(#splashDropGrad)\"`, `stroke`, and `filter`, the micro-droplets now appear as refractive clear water with delicate shadows against a white background, which is exactly the water droplet aesthetic I\'m aiming for. Next, I\'ll work on defining how the splash bursts are emitted with a function helper.\n\n\n**Defining Splash Ejection Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the burst emission function. I\'m calculating a particle count based on impact speed and spread. The algorithm uses the surface normal for a semi-circular spread, with each particle\'s velocity scaled according to the impact. I\'m also adding minor random variations in particle velocity, initial radius, and opacity, and considering maximum lifetimes.\n\n\n**Implementing Particle Pool Limits**\n\nI\'ve introduced a particle pool limit within the `triggerSplash` function to control the maximum number of splash particles, preventing excessive memory usage. I\'m now managing the pool by capping its size to 120, removing the oldest particles when the limit is reached. Next, I\'ll be working on fine-tuning this limit and assessing its performance impact.\n\n\n**Analyzing Collision Physics**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the collision response calculations. Right now I\'m focusing on defining how the water droplets interact with different surface scenarios: the ceiling, and the walls. I have implemented a mechanism to simulate the physics when a droplet encounters the ceiling, including adjusting velocity, and triggering a splash. I\'m also planning out similar methods for the left and right walls.\n\n\n**Refining Wall Collision Dynamics**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the wall collision dynamics. I\'ve successfully implemented left-wall collision with impact sounds and splash triggers, including squash effects. Now, I\'m working to extend this to the right wall, ensuring a symmetrical and realistic response. I\'m tweaking the `triggerSplash` function to handle diverse wall contact points, and evaluating the collision response based on impact speed and restitution.\n\n\n**Expanding Collision Interactions**\n\nI\'m now exploring scenarios beyond wall collisions, specifically droplet-on-droplet interactions. I\'m focusing on defining collision logic where relative speed is high, emitting a tiny micro-splash at the contact point. I\'m taking into account user feedback regarding splash effects on edges, so I am adjusting the triggerSplash function to handle the floor and screen edges.\n\n\n**Integrating Particle Physics**\n\nI\'ve integrated the particle physics into the RAF loop. The loop now updates and renders splash particles, calculating ballistic parabolic trajectories with gravity and air drag. Each frame manages droplet life, shrinking radii, and fading opacity.\n\n\n"
