  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Splash Logic**\n\nI\'m currently structuring a helper function named `triggerSplash`. Its purpose will be to manage the visual effects of a splash. The function will take several inputs: origin coordinates, a normal vector representing the splash direction, impact speed, and a scale factor. This is where I\'m at, as it stands.\n\n\n**Calculating Particle Parameters**\n\nI\'ve been working on the particle count within `triggerSplash`. It is determined dynamically by impact speed and scale. The `baseAngle` for particle direction is calculated, and then I\'m generating individual particles with randomized spreads, speeds, radii, and lifetimes. Each particle\'s properties are also being initialized.\n\n\n**Refining Particle Management**\n\nI\'m now managing particle limits within `triggerSplash`. I\'ve added a check to ensure the particle array doesn\'t exceed 120 elements. If it does, I\'m trimming the array from the beginning. Additionally, I\'m integrating this logic with floor collision detection; I\'m calling the function when an object hits the floor, and I\'m ensuring proper particle ejection upon impact.\n\n\n**Implementing Collision Effects**\n\nI\'m now integrating collision responses for the droplets, starting with ceiling impacts. I\'m focusing on handling `impactSpeed` and triggering `playImpactSound` based on the impact force. Also, I\'m calculating and applying `squashPower` to create a more dynamic collision effect. This affects `wobbleVel` parameters, and I\'m also triggering `triggerSplash` based on the impact speed. I\'m also planning out similar logic for wall collisions.\n\n\n**Expanding Collision Response**\n\nI\'m now expanding collision responses for walls. I\'m focusing on handling `impactSpeed` and triggering `playImpactSound`, especially based on force, calculating and applying `squashPower`. I\'m also planning out similar logic for wall collisions. Finally, I\'m integrating the same splash logic I had for the left wall onto the right wall.\n\n\n**Simulating Particle Trajectories**\n\nI\'m now integrating particle trajectory updates within the requestAnimationFrame loop. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the splash particles. Each frame, I\'m updating their life, applying a ballistic parabolic trajectory with gravity and gentle air drag. Also, I\'m removing dead particles from the array, keeping it efficient.\n\n\n**Updating Particle Display**\n\nI\'m now integrating particle rendering within the `requestAnimationFrame` loop. I\'m focusing on the visual aspect of the splash particles and updating their positions, radii, and opacity on each frame. This is also where I\'m optimizing the process of displaying and hiding SVG elements, using an SVG context and associated function calls to manage the visualization within the particle array.\n\n\n**Optimizing Visuals**\n\nI\'m now integrating optimized particle rendering, focusing on the visual aspects within the return statement. I\'m focusing on rendering splashes of white, semi-transparent particles using SVG elements. I am currently rendering 90 particles per splash, utilizing `radialGradient` for the colors and `feDropShadow` filter for a more realistic effect.\n\n\n"
