    thinking: "**Analyzing Ripple Implementation**\n\nI\'ve been examining the ripple effect\'s implementation, focusing specifically on lines 1228-1240. I see ripples are exclusively triggered by `onClick` events within a 1:1 window. This initial understanding provides a baseline for investigating its behavior and potential modifications.\n\n\n**Implementing Ripple Trigger**\n\nI\'ve shifted focus to incorporating ripple animations during droplet collisions. I\'m focusing on incorporating a sound effect when a collision is detected. Right now, I can see how `getBoundingClientRect()` accurately computes the location where a ripple will appear. I will next look at the ripple effect\'s position, as it relates to the droplets. I\'ve begun to sketch how the code should adjust the \"setRipples\" function to trigger from collision detection events.\n\n\n**Defining Ripple Origination**\n\nI\'m now determining the origin point for the ripples. I\'ve broken this down for four scenarios: collisions with the floor, ceiling, left, and right walls. I\'m calculating the correct coordinates for these edge collisions. For droplet-droplet collisions, I\'m working through the contact point calculation, deriving the necessary contact vector from the normalized distance between droplets.\n\n\n**Refining Ripple Animation**\n\nI\'m now focused on the visual appeal of the ripple. I\'ve been experimenting with a custom CSS animation using `@keyframes`. I\'ve started defining a `waterSurfaceRipple` animation. I\'m aiming for subtle, organic behavior. I\'ve adjusted the initial scale, opacity, and border to achieve a more natural water-like effect and improve the animation\'s look.\n\n\n**Optimizing Ripple Visuals**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual aesthetics, refining the ripple\'s appearance. The box shadow is the key; `0 0 6px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8)` gives a bright refractive crest. `inset 0 0 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12)` defines the trough, and a translucent `border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95)` adds the outer ring, creating an authentic wave ring expansion across the droplet\'s meniscus.\n\n\n**Managing Ripple Quantity**\n\nI\'m now focusing on controlling the number of active ripples. I\'m exploring limits like keeping a maximum of 12 ripples. I\'m considering cleaning up ripples after 850ms, also. I\'ve been examining the `App.tsx` file for the `ripples` state and the `WindowRipple` interface. I\'m checking the `lastRippleTime` property of `DropletState`, and I have been trying to limit ripples when droplets collide. I\'m still trying to decide between React state management versus DOM injection.\n\n\n**Evaluating DOM Injection**\n\nThe DOM manipulation approach is looking promising; it provides significant performance benefits. Creating and removing elements directly seems like a more streamlined and efficient method, especially considering the high frequency of collisions. I\'m focusing on the trade-offs now and confirming it works cleanly with `cardElementsRef`. It seems like DOM creation is the key for a very smooth visual experience!\n\n\n**Refining Ripple Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the ripple effects directly into the DOM structure. Each card will contain the droplet\'s content and a ripple span. The ripples, absolutely positioned, are `pointer-events-none` to avoid interference. With `overflow: hidden` on the `.card`, the ripple\'s animation expands organically within the droplet\'s meniscus. The CSS handles the GPU-accelerated ripple animations, creating a visually appealing effect. The keyframe animation I\'m employing is defined precisely. I\'m focusing on ensuring this integration is seamless and efficient.\n\n\n**Defining Ripple Properties**\n\nI\'m now detailing the characteristics of the ripple effect, starting with the CSS. I\'ve re-reviewed the animation\'s timing functions, ensuring the cubic-bezier curve creates a smooth visual experience. The scale and size adjustments are precisely configured: the initial scale and expansion at 100% are now accurate for the droplet size. The refractive border and shadow effects are also dialed in. I am confirming it is positioned correctly.\n\n\n**Calculating Ripple Placement**\n\nI\'m now precisely calculating ripple positions for various collision scenarios. For bottom collisions, the origin is at (110, 195); top at (110, 25); left at (25, 110); right at (195, 110). Droplet-to-droplet collisions use the normal vector to determine contact points for ripple origination on both droplets. I am working through the merging scenario now, using (110 + nx * 85, 110 + ny * 85) for the dominant droplet.\n\n\n**Implementing Ripple Handling**\n\nI\'ve added collision ripple triggers, leveraging normalized contact vectors for precise origin calculations. Ripple throttling using `lastRippleTime` prevents excessive animations on rapid collisions. The `triggerDropletRipple` function handles the creation of ripple spans and throttles triggers. The `onClick` events still work, seamlessly integrated into the code. I am confirming ripple placement and throttling.\n\n\n\n"
