  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Implementing Device Orientation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing a device orientation listener. My goal is to use this data to subtly accelerate the droplets, simulating a natural sliding effect based on the device\'s tilt. I will prioritize a subtle implementation for a more realistic feel.\n\n\n**Analyzing Device Orientation Events**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `DeviceOrientationEvent` to understand how it works in browsers and mobile web environments. I plan to use this information to create a natural sliding effect for the droplets in the scene, using the device\'s tilt as input.\n\n\n**Understanding Tilt Angles**\n\nI\'ve learned about the key properties of `DeviceOrientationEvent`: `gamma` (left/right tilt) and `beta` (forward/backward tilt). The beta angle is from -180 to +180. I\'ve also identified an iOS 13+ permission requirement: `requestPermission()` must be triggered by a user gesture. This aligns perfectly with the current `onPointerDown` gesture.\n\n\n**Calculating Tilt Effects**\n\nI\'m now calculating how to apply the tilt effect. I\'m focusing on clamping gamma to -45 to 45 degrees for a smooth experience. I\'m normalizing this to a tilt value, from -1 to 1. Beta tilt changes the screen orientation. When the device is upright, the normalized beta value would be +1. In other words, I\'m working with sine to calculate the gravity vector decomposition, and verifying the expected behavior.\n\n\n**Decomposing Gravity Vectors**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how device tilt directly affects the simulated gravity. Rightward tilt means positive `g_x`, accelerating droplets towards the right wall. The more upright I hold the phone, the stronger `g_y`\'s downward pull. Conversely, tilting forward reverses `g_y`, pulling upwards. Lying flat effectively nullifies horizontal and vertical gravity.\n\n\n**Refining Gravity Calculations**\n\nI\'m now refining the gravity calculation to use `sin(gammaRad)` and `sin(betaRad)` for `ax` and `ay`, and then smooth it with a low-pass filter, a lerp factor between 0.1 and 0.15. This is to reduce noise and provide a silky-smooth effect for the water motion, responding realistically to device tilt.\nI\'m also considering how to integrate this tilt feature in preview environments, adding mouse tilt preview when hovering near edges for desktop users, while mobile users can still use device orientation.\n\n\n"
