    thinking: "**Analyzing Device Orientation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on how to best implement a device orientation listener. My initial thought is to apply a subtle acceleration to the droplets, reflecting the device\'s tilt. I\'m exploring the specifics of this acceleration, and I\'ll see what the user requests from the first request right before compaction.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Controls**\n\nI\'ve successfully integrated the device orientation listener and am currently fine-tuning the physics engine, focusing on smoother droplet movement via `gravityX` and `gravityY`. Furthermore, I\'ve prioritized the recent user requests and I\'m now actively adding the \'Evaporation Rate\' control. I\'m focusing on incorporating a slider in the water-taskbar and linking it to a state ref for dynamic evaporation scaling, to implement that user feature from the latest requests.\n\n\n**Refining Aesthetic Controls**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the user experience. I\'m prioritizing UX styling rules to maintain a clean visual hierarchy and prevent any visual clutter, to preserve the minimalist water aesthetic. I aim to create a custom water droplet-styled slider for the taskbar, avoiding jarring HUD elements. I\'m focusing on feature and design perfection. I\'m also finishing the Device Orientation acceleration in `src/App.tsx` and updating `drop.vx` and `drop.vy`.\n\n\n**Optimizing Particle Interactions**\n\nI\'m now integrating sophisticated collision handling, ensuring droplets slide realistically across surfaces when the device is tilted. I\'ve implemented conditional checks within collision logic based on `gravityX` and `gravityY` to achieve this natural sliding and lift-off. Additionally, I\'ve updated the splash particle physics to include `gravityX` and `gravityY` for dynamic splash behavior.\n\n\n**Implementing Evaporation Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the evaporation logic, linking it to the slider control, and testing various edge cases. I\'m focusing on the condition where a droplet becomes inactive for a short time. I\'ve successfully implemented slow mass/volume loss via `evaporationRate`. I\'ve also incorporated the evaporation trigger when `drop.scale < 0.28`. I\'m now fine-tuning the `speedMult` for more dynamic dissolution and adjusting the value range for the evaporation rate slider, specifically from 0 (off) to 1.0 (fast).\n\n\n**Designing Evaporation Slider**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the UI implementation of the evaporation rate slider. I\'m focusing on crafting a water droplet-themed slider for the taskbar to preserve the minimalist aesthetic. I\'m considering initial default values and range settings for the evaporation rate slider, and considering edge cases for `evaporationRate`. I\'ve also incorporated the evaporation trigger when `drop.scale < 0.28`. I\'m now fine-tuning the `speedMult` for more dynamic dissolution and adjusting the value range for the evaporation rate slider.\n\n\n**Crafting Liquid Slider**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on designing the evaporation rate slider for the UI, striving for a water-themed aesthetic. I envision a liquid-capsule track for the slider, with a minimalist water channel and inset shadow. The thumb will be a refractive water droplet. I\'ll include a water vapor icon with a tooltip showing the evaporation rate. I\'ll implement a range input with Tailwind and custom CSS to render the liquid meniscus slider track and the droplet thumb. I\'m considering the state and ref management for the slider.\n\n\n"
